{"obligations":{"reduction":{"status":"written_and_valid","quote":null,"witness":null,"load_bearing":null},"lower_bound":{"status":"not_written","quote":"**Proof.** Induction over the sequence terms in increasing order (standard; details omitted). \(\square\)","witness":"The missing statement is an all-terms proof that for every sequence term x, its support on primes at most A meets the prime support of every sequence term. Merely naming induction and omitting its induction step does not prove the large-prime-erasure lemma; the later mention of an unshown construction Rp^k does not supply that proof.","load_bearing":"This lemma is the sole justification for deleting all primes greater than A. Without it, membership may still depend on infinitely many primes."},"upper_bound":{"status":"not_written","quote":"Hence \(\sigma(m+L)=\sigma(m)\), and (3) gives
\[
m\text{ is a sequence term}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
m+L\text{ is a sequence term}. \tag{4}
\]","witness":"The required all-cases conclusion is unconditional membership invariance under m↦m+L for every m≥A, followed by the residue count yielding the index shift from n=1. The displayed inference proves this only through (3), whose forward direction depends entirely on the omitted large-prime-erasure lemma, so no all-cases proof of (4) is written.","load_bearing":"Equation (4) is essential both to make a_n+L a term and to show every length-L window contains exactly T terms. Without a proved (4), the final index identity does not follow."}},"S_valid":0,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"The characterization is proved correctly, and the proposed L and T are explicit. The central finiteness lemma is wholly omitted, making the otherwise coherent periodicity assembly conditional on an unproved assertion."}
